<DataShopLayout current="mission" title="Mission | DATA" description="Why Dharmic Data makes cultural objects with evidence attached.">
  <section class="page-hero mission-hero">
    <p class="overline">Why DATA exists</p>
    <h1>Culture is data<br />with a pulse.</h1>
    <p>Dharmic Data builds tools and artifacts that help people orient quickly, inspect the evidence, and make a conscious next decision.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="manifesto">
    <blockquote>“Build, then show the work.”</blockquote>
    <div>
      <p>DATA applies that principle to cultural memory. Instead of flattening a joke into a slogan, we preserve the path from source to remix: the map, the archive, the object, and the conversation around it.</p>
      <p>The result is not a neutral database and not disposable merchandise. It is a legible point of view with enough context for someone else to examine it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="principles">
    <article><span>01</span><h2>Evidence before scale</h2><p>We prototype in public, test recognition, and manufacture only after demand becomes real.</p></article>
    <article><span>02</span><h2>Context stays attached</h2><p>Each edition links back to its dossier so the story remains available after the drop ends.</p></article>
    <article><span>03</span><h2>People keep authority</h2><p>Technology can reveal a pattern; people decide what the pattern means and what happens next.</p></article>
    <article><span>04</span><h2>Limits are visible</h2><p>Satire, archives, and generated imagery carry legal and interpretive limits. We name them instead of hiding them.</p></article>
  </section>
  <section class="cta-band">
    <p>See the principle in motion.</p>
    <a href="/atlas/">Open the Pied-à-Pierre Atlas <span>↗</span></a>
  </section>
</DataShopLayout>
